### 3. Peaceful Cooperation Metric for Regional Hubs

Define a bilateral (or multi-lateral) **Cooperative Fidelity Index** between hubs \(i\) and \(j\):

\[
C_{ij}(t) = \underbrace{\frac{\eta_{ij}^{\text{shared}}}{\eta_i + \eta_j - \eta_{ij}^{\text{shared}}}}_{\text{information coupling}} \times \underbrace{\left(1 - \frac{S_{\text{conflict},ij}}{S_{\text{max}}}\right)}_{\text{conflict entropy reduction}} \times \underbrace{\frac{K_{ij}}{\max(K_i,K_j)}}_{\text{knowledge capital sharing}}
\]

where
- \(\eta_{ij}^{\text{shared}}\) = fidelity of jointly operated or mutually accessible compute/knowledge resources,
- \(S_{\text{conflict},ij}\) = measured conflict entropy (export controls, military expenditure directed against the other, information warfare intensity, etc.),
- \(K_{ij}\) = volume of high-fidelity knowledge capital exchanged under open or reciprocal licenses.

A regional hub (e.g., Singapore–Johor–Batam, or an ASEAN compute corridor) is ZEM-compliant when
\[
\min_{j \in \text{partners}} C_{ij}(t) \ge C_{\text{threshold}} \quad \text{and} \quad \frac{dC_{ij}}{dt} \ge 0
\]
for all significant partners \(j\).

This metric converts the abstract call to “redirect priorities from militarization to peaceful cooperation” into an operational, measurable quantity that can be tracked in real time by both human and SI agents.

